{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050122 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115226 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115056 :question/math-academy-id "e-4411" :question/problem "Find $AB-CB,$ where\n\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix}, \\quad B=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & \\sqrt{3} & -45 \\\\ 81 & -\\sqrt{5} & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}, \\quad C=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -4 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "We can apply the distributive law\n\n$$\nAB-CB=(A-C)B.\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $A-C$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-C & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -4 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-{I}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB-CB & =(A-C)B \\\\  & =-{I}_{3}B \\\\  & =-B \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{2} & -\\sqrt{3} & 45 \\\\ -81 & \\sqrt{5} & 2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & -\\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115829 :question/math-academy-id "q-42004" :question/problem "Calculate $BA + BC$, where\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -5 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}, B = \\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & \\sqrt{3} & 1 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} & \\sqrt{6} & 1 \\\\ \\sqrt{7} & \\sqrt{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}, C = \\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115836 :question/math-academy-id "q-42263" :question/problem "Find $AB+CB$, where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -5 & -1 \\\\ -9 & 10 & 11\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & 1 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{5} & 1 \\\\ \\sqrt{7} & 1 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 9 & -10 & -13\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the distributive law\n\n$$\nAB+CB=(A+C)B.\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $A+C$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+C & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -5 & -1 \\\\ -9 & 10 & 11\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 9 & -10 & -13\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-2{I}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB+CB & =(A+C)B \\\\  & =-2{I}_{3}B \\\\  & =-2B \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2\\sqrt{2} & -2 & -2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ -2 & -2\\sqrt{5} & -2 \\\\ -2\\sqrt{7} & -2 & -2\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235392 :question/math-academy-id "q-138783" :question/problem "$$\nX+Y=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,Z=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Knowing that the matrix representation $T=\\begin{bmatrix}{t}_{11} & {t}_{12} \\\\ {t}_{21} & {t}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$ of a linear transformation $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=XZ+YZ,\n$$\n\nfind the values ${t}_{ij}.$\n\n${t}_{11}=$ {{field-1}}\n\n${t}_{12}=$ {{field-2}}\n\n${t}_{21}=$ {{field-3}}\n\n${t}_{22}=$ {{field-4}}" :question/worked-solution "We apply the distributive law\n\n$$\nXZ+YZ=(X+Y)Z.\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =XZ+YZ \\\\  & =(X+Y)Z \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265870 :question/math-academy-id "q-130269" :question/problem "Calculate $BA+BC$, where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -5 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & \\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{7} \\\\ \\sqrt{5} & \\sqrt{6} & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{7} & \\sqrt{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 5 & -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We can apply the distributive law\n\n$$\nBA+BC=B(A+C).\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $A+C$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+C & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -5 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 5 & -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BA+BC & =B(A+C) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & \\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{7} \\\\ \\sqrt{5} & \\sqrt{6} & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{7} & \\sqrt{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292858 :question/math-academy-id "q-275515" :question/problem "Calculate $AB+AC$, where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 3 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n|  |  | $4$ | $3$ | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $AB+AC=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} | $0$ | $-2$ |  |\n|  |  | $-1$ | {{field-3}} | $5$ |  |" :question/worked-solution "We apply the distributive law \n\n$$\nAB+AC=A(B+C).\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $B+C$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B+C & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 3 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB+AC & =A(B+C) \\\\  & =A{I}_{3} \\\\  & =A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292859 :question/math-academy-id "q-275518" :question/problem "Find $AC+BC$, where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -6 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 8 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{5} & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3} & 4 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n|  |  | $8\\sqrt{5}$ | $-4$ | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $AC+BC=\\,\\,$ |  | $0$ | {{field-2}} | $16$ |  |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} | $12$ | $20$ |  |" :question/worked-solution "We apply the distributive law\n\n$$\nAC+BC=(A+B)C.\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $A+B$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+B & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -6 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 8 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =4{I}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC+BC & =(A+B)C \\\\  & =4{I}_{3}C \\\\  & =4C \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8\\sqrt{5} & -4 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 4\\sqrt{3} & 16 \\\\ -8 & 12 & 20\\text{ }\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299802 :question/math-academy-id "q-319886" :question/problem "Evaluate $AC+BC$, where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -4\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & \\sqrt{6} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n|  |  | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $AC+BC=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | $0$ | $0$ |  |\n|  |  | $3$ | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |  |" :question/worked-solution "We can apply the distributive law\n\n$$\nAC+BC=(A+B)C.\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $A+B$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+B & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -4\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC+BC & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & \\sqrt{6} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 3\\sqrt{6}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299803 :question/math-academy-id "q-319890" :question/problem "Calculate $AB+AC$, where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & \\sqrt{3}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n|  |  | $0$ | $0$ | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $AB+AC=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} | $\\sqrt{3}$ | $2$ |  |\n|  |  | $0$ | {{field-3}} | $0$ |  |" :question/worked-solution "We can apply the distributive law\n\n$$\nAB+AC=A(B+C).\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $B+C$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B+C & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & \\sqrt{3}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & \\sqrt{3} & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB+AC & =A(B+C) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & \\sqrt{3} & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & \\sqrt{3} & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299807 :question/math-academy-id "q-319917" :question/problem "Calculate $CB-CA$, where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -5\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n|  |  | $0$ | {{field-1}} | $0$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $CB-CA=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} | $0$ | {{field-3}} |  |\n|  |  | $0$ | {{field-4}} | $0$ |  |" :question/worked-solution "We apply the distributive law\n\n$$\nCB-CA=C(B-A).\n$$\n\nSo, compute $B-A$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -5\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-3{I}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CB-CA & =C(B-A) \\\\  & =C(-3{I}_{3}) \\\\  & =-3C \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299808 :question/math-academy-id "q-319922" :question/problem "Calculate $AC-BC$, where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -4\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n|  |  | $4$ | $0$ | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $AC-BC=\\,\\,$ |  | $0$ | $-4$ | {{field-2}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} | $4$ |  |" :question/worked-solution "We apply the distributive law on the right:\n\n$$\nAC-BC=(A-B)C.\n$$\n\nSo, compute $A-B$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-B & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =4{I}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC-BC & =(A-B)C \\\\  & =(4{I}_{3})C \\\\  & =4C \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345111 :question/math-academy-id "q-75635" :question/problem "Calculate $CB+CA$, where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ -1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & -\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -\\sqrt{3} & -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the distributive law \n\n$$\nCB+CA=C(B+A).\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $B+A$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B+A & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & -\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -\\sqrt{3} & -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ -1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =4{I}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CB+CA & =C(B+A) \\\\  & =C\\cdot 4{I}_{3} \\\\  & =4C \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388090 :question/math-academy-id "q-130259" :question/problem "Calculate $AC+BC$, where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & \\sqrt{2} & 1 \\\\ 1 & -\\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We can apply the distributive law\n\n$$\nAC+BC=(A+B)C.\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $A+B$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+B & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC+BC & =(A+B)C \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & \\sqrt{2} & 1 \\\\ 1 & -\\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & \\sqrt{2} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485535 :question/math-academy-id "q-130275" :question/problem "Find $AB+CB$, where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -8 & 9 & 9\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 1 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 3 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -2\\sqrt{7} & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 8 & -9 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the distributive law\n\n$$\nAB+CB=(A+C)B.\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $A+C$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+C & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -8 & 9 & 9\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 8 & -9 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =5{I}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB+CB & =(A+C)B \\\\  & =5{I}_{3}B \\\\  & =5B \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}40 & 5 & 5\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 15 & 25 & 5 \\\\ -10\\sqrt{7} & 5 & 15\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485538 :question/math-academy-id "q-319197" :question/problem "Calculate $AC+BC$, where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -4\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n|  |  | $2$ | {{field-1}} | $0$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $AC+BC=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} | $4$ | $2$ |  |\n|  |  | $5$ | {{field-3}} | $3$ |  |" :question/worked-solution "We can apply the distributive law\n\n$$\nAC+BC=(A+B)C.\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $A+B$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+B & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC+BC & =(A+B)C \\\\  & ={I}_{3}\\cdot C \\\\  & =C \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485543 :question/math-academy-id "q-75612" :question/problem "Find $AC+BC$, where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -6\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 6 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 9\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & \\sqrt{2} & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{3} & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2 & \\sqrt{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the distributive law\n\n$$\nAC+BC=(A+B)C.\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $A+B$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+B & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -6\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 6 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =3{I}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC+BC & =(A+B)C \\\\  & =3{I}_{3}C \\\\  & =3C \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3\\sqrt{2} & 3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 6 & 3\\sqrt{3} & 6 \\\\ 9 & 6 & 3\\sqrt{5}\\text{ }\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535152 :question/problem "$$\nX+Y=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,Z=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Knowing that the matrix representation $T=\\begin{bmatrix}{t}_{11} & {t}_{12} \\\\ {t}_{21} & {t}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$ of a linear transformation $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=XZ+YZ,\n$$\n\nfind the values ${t}_{ij}.$\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n${t}_{11}=$\n\n\n\n\n\n{{field-1}}                \n\n\n\n\n\n$\\,{t}_{12}=$\n\n\n\n\n\n{{field-2}}                \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n${t}_{21}=$\n\n\n\n\n\n{{field-3}}                \n\n\n\n\n\n$\\,{t}_{22}=$\n\n\n\n\n\n{{field-4}}" :question/worked-solution "We apply the distributive law\n\n$$\nXZ+YZ=(X+Y)Z.\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =XZ+YZ \\\\  & =(X+Y)Z \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Matrix Product Using the Distributive Law"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115227 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115057 :question/math-academy-id "e-4412" :question/problem "Calculate $ABC,$ where\n\n\n$$\nA= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, \\quad B= \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}, \\quad C= \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC = (AB)C = A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix, $B$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix, and $C$ is a $3 \\times 1$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $3 \\times 3$ matrix that will require finding $9$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $3 \\times 1$ matrix that will require finding only $3$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $BC$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $A$ by $BC\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =A(BC) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115828 :question/math-academy-id "q-41785" :question/problem "Calculate $ABC$, where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -4 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix, $B$ is a $3\\times 1$ matrix, and $C$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $2\\times 1$ matrix, which will require finding $2$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $3\\times 2$ matrix, which will require finding $6$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $AB$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $AB$ by $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =(AB)C \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 15 \\\\ 3 & -9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115835 :question/math-academy-id "q-42257" :question/problem "Calculate $ABC$, where\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}, B = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -5 & -5\\end{bmatrix}, C = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265880 :question/math-academy-id "q-130313" :question/problem "Calculate $ABC$, where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix, $B$ is a $2\\times 1$ matrix, and $C$ is a $1\\times 3$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $3\\times 1$ matrix, which will require finding $3$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $2\\times 3$ matrix, which will require finding $6$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $AB$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $AB$ by $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =(AB)C \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -3 & -9 \\\\ -8 & 8 & 24 \\\\ 7 & -7 & -21\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292860 :question/math-academy-id "q-275521" :question/problem "Calculate $ABC$, where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n| $ABC=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix, $B$ is a $3\\times 1$ matrix, and $C$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $2\\times 1$ matrix, which will require finding $2$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $3\\times 2$ matrix, which will require finding $6$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $AB$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $AB$ by $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =(AB)C \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-11 & 11 \\\\ -8 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299810 :question/math-academy-id "q-319938" :question/problem "Calculate $ABC$, where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $ABC=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $3\\times 1$ matrix, $B$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix, and $C$ is a $2\\times 1$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $3\\times 2$ matrix, which will require finding $6$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $1\\times 1$ matrix, which will require finding only $1$ entry.\n\nSo, we find the product $BC$ first: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-13\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $A$ by $BC:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =A(BC) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-13\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-65 \\\\ 13 \\\\ -39\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299811 :question/math-academy-id "q-319943" :question/problem "Calculate $ABC$, where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $ABC=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $3\\times 1$ matrix, $B$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix, and $C$ is a $2\\times 1$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $3\\times 2$ matrix, which will require finding $6$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $1\\times 1$ matrix, which will require finding only $1$ entry.\n\nSo, we find the product $BC$ first: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-20-6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-26\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $A$ by $BC:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =A(BC) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-26\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}78 \\\\ -52 \\\\ -182\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299814 :question/math-academy-id "q-320021" :question/problem "Calculate $ABC$, where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" rowspan=\"2\">$ABC=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix, $B$ is a $2\\times 1$ matrix, and $C$ is a $1\\times 3$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $1\\times 1$ matrix, which will require finding only $1$ entry, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $2\\times 3$ matrix, which will require finding $6$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $AB$ first: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6\\cdot (-1)+(-2)\\cdot 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6-6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-12\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $(AB)$ by $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =(AB)\\,C \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-12\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-24 & 48 & -60\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299815 :question/math-academy-id "q-320026" :question/problem "Calculate $ABC$, where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 6\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n| $ABC=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix, $B$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix, and $C$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $1\\times 2$ matrix, which will require finding $2$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $2\\times 2$ matrix, which will require finding $4$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $AB$ first: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 34\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $AB$ by $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =(AB)C \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 34\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-58 & 136\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326920 :question/math-academy-id "q-42001" :question/problem "Calculate $ABC$, where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix, $B$ is a $2\\times 1$ matrix, and $C$ is a $1\\times 3$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $3\\times 1$ matrix, which will require finding $3$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $2\\times 3$ matrix, which will require finding $6$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $AB$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -21 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $AB$ by $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =(AB)C \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -21 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-18 & 0 & 9 \\\\ 42 & 0 & -21 \\\\ 8 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326921 :question/math-academy-id "q-42002" :question/problem "Calculate $ABC$, where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $3\\times 1$ matrix, $B$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix, and $C$ is a $2\\times 1$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $3\\times 2$ matrix, which will require finding $6$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $1\\times 1$ matrix, which will require finding only $1$ entry.\n\nSo, we find the product $BC$ first: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $A$ by $BC:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =A(BC) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ 24 \\\\ 36\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392448 :question/math-academy-id "q-41981" :question/problem "Calculate $ABC$, where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix, $B$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix, and $C$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $1\\times 2$ matrix, which will require finding $2$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $2\\times 2$ matrix, which will require finding $4$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $AB$ first: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 11\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $AB$ by $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =(AB)C \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 11\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392501 :question/math-academy-id "q-42258" :question/problem "Calculate $ABC$, where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix, $B$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix, and $C$ is a $3\\times 1$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $3\\times 3$ matrix, which will require finding $9$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $2\\times 1$ matrix, which will require finding $2$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $BC$ first: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $A$ by $BC:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =A(BC) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-68 \\\\ -22 \\\\ 50\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392508 :question/math-academy-id "q-42259" :question/problem "Calculate $ABC$, where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix, $B$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix, and $C$ is a $3\\times 1$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $3\\times 3$ matrix, which will require finding $9$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $2\\times 1$ matrix, which will require finding $2$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $BC$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $A$ by $BC:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =A(BC) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408070 :question/math-academy-id "q-130322" :question/problem "Calculate $ABC$, where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix, $B$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix, and $C$ is a $3\\times 1$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $3\\times 3$ matrix, which will require finding $9$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $2\\times 1$ matrix, which will require finding $2$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $BC$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $A$ by $BC:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =A(BC) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-14 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 28\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485578 :question/math-academy-id "q-320017" :question/problem "Calculate $ABC$, where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $ABC=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix, $B$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix, and $C$ is a $3\\times 1$ matrix. Therefore,\n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$ gives a $3\\times 3$ matrix, which would require finding $9$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$ gives a $2\\times 1$ matrix, which requires finding $2$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $BC$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $A$ by $BC:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =A(BC) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -15 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549231 :question/problem "Calculate $ABC$, where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -4 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n| $ABC=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix, $B$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix, and $C$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $1\\times 2$ matrix, which will require finding $2$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $2\\times 2$ matrix, which will require finding $4$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $AB$ first: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-12 & -24\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $AB$ by $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =(AB)C \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-12 & -24\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 & -4 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 96\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549239 :question/problem "Calculate $ABC$, where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -4\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n| $ABC=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix, $B$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix, and $C$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $1\\times 2$ matrix, which will require finding $2$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $2\\times 2$ matrix, which will require finding $4$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $AB$ first: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}6 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-20 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $AB$ by $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =(AB)C \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-20 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-110 & -110\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549246 :question/problem "Calculate $ABC$, where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" rowspan=\"2\">$ABC=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix, $B$ is a $2\\times 1$ matrix, and $C$ is a $1\\times 3$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $1\\times 1$ matrix, which will require finding only $1$ entry, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $2\\times 3$ matrix, which will require finding $6$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $AB$ first: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0\\cdot 4+(-3)\\cdot (-6)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0+18\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}18\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $(AB)$ by $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =(AB)\\,C \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}18\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}54 & 0 & 72\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Matrix Product Using the Associative Law"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115228 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115058 :question/math-academy-id "e-4413" :question/problem "Given that $A, B,$ and $C$ are $6 \\times 6$ matrices, which of the following statements are always true?\n\nI. $A(BC) = (AB)C$\n\nII. $A(B+C) = BA+CA$\n\nIII. $A(B+C) = AB+AC$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Matrix multiplication is associative, so $(AB)C = A(BC)$ in general.\n\n- Statement II is false. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so $A(B+C) = AB + AC$ in general. However, since matrix multiplication is not commutative, we have\n\n$$\nAB + AC  \\neq BA + CA.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so $(A+B)C = AC+BC,$ and $A(B+C) = AB+AC$ in general.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true."} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115837 :question/math-academy-id "q-42265" :question/problem "Given that $A,B,$ and $C$ are $4\\times 4$ matrices, which of the following statements are always true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $AB=BA$\n- **II.** $(AB)C=A(BC)$\n- **III.** $AC+BC=(A+B)C$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. Matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $AB\\ne BA$ in general.\n\n- Statement II is true. Matrix multiplication is associative, so $(AB)C=A(BC)$ in general.\n\n- Statement III is true. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so $(A+B)C=AC+BC,$ and $A(B+C)=AB+AC$ in general.\n\nTherefore, only statements II and III are true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115838 :question/math-academy-id "q-42266" :question/problem "Given that $A,B,$ and $C$ are $6\\times 6$ matrices, which of the following statements are always true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $(A+B)C=AC+BC$\n- **II.** $AB=BA$\n- **III.** $A(BC)=(BA)C$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so $(A+B)C=AC+BC,$ and $A(B+C)=AB+AC$ in general.\n\n- Statement II is false. Matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $AB\\ne BA$ in general.\n\n- Statement III is false. Matrix multiplication is associative, so $(AB)C=A(BC)$ in general. However, since matrix multiplication is not commutative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(BC) &=(AB)C\\ne (BA)C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265906 :question/math-academy-id "q-130446" :question/problem "Given that $A,B,$ and $C$ are $4\\times 4$ matrices which of the following statements are always true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${A}^{2}B=A(AB)$\n- **II.** $A(B-C)=AB-CA$\n- **III.** $B(CA)=(CB)A$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Matrix multiplication is associative and  ${A}^{2}B=AAB=A(AB)$ is always true.\n\n- Statement II is false.  Matrix multiplication is distributive and  $A(B-C)=AB-AC.$ However, matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $AC\\ne CA$ in general and therefore $A(B-C)\\ne AB-CA$, in general.\n\n- Statement III is false. Matrix multiplication is associative and  $B(CA)=(BC)A.$ However, matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $BC\\ne CB$ in general and therefore $B(CA)\\ne (CB)A$, in general.\n\nTherefore, only statement  I is true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265907 :question/math-academy-id "q-130449" :question/problem "Given that $A,B,$ and $C$ are $3\\times 3$ matrices, and  ${I}_{3}$ is the $3\\times 3$ identity matrix, which of the following statements are always true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $AB=A{I}_{3}B$\n- **II.** $A(B+{I}_{3})=AB$\n- **III.** ${I}_{3}A=A{I}_{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Matrix multiplication is associative, so $A{I}_{3}B=(A{I}_{3})B=AB.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so $A(B+{I}_{3})=AB+A{I}_{3}=AB+A.$ However,  $AB+A\\ne AB,$ in general.\n\n- Statement III is true. Matrix multiplication is not commutative in general, however, multiplication by the identity matrix is always commutative. In this case, we have  ${I}_{3}A=A{I}_{3}.$\n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265909 :question/math-academy-id "q-130455" :question/problem "Given that $A,B,$ and $C$ are $6\\times 6$ matrices which of the following statements are always true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A(BC)=(AB)C$\n\n- **II.** $A(B-C)=AB-CA$\n\n- **III.** $CA=AC$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Matrix multiplication is associative and  $A(BC)=(AB)C$ is always true.\n\n- Statement II is false. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so $A(B-C)=AB-AC,$ but $AC\\ne CA$ because matrix multiplication is not commutative in general.\n\n- Statement III  is false. Matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $CA\\ne AC$ in general.\n\nTherefore, only statement  I is true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265913 :question/math-academy-id "q-130463" :question/problem "Given that $A$ and $B$  are $8\\times 8$ matrices,  and ${I}_{8}$ is the $8\\times 8$ identity matrix,  which of the following statements are always true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A{I}_{8}={I}_{8}A$\n- **II.** $AB=BA$\n- **III.** $A\\cdot 2{I}_{8}=2A$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Matrix multiplication is not commutative in general, however, multiplication by the identity matrix is always commutative. In this case, we have  $A{I}_{8}={I}_{8}A=A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $AB\\ne BA$ in general.\n\n- Statement III is true. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\cdot 2{I}_{8} & =A({I}_{8}+{I}_{8}) \\\\  & =A{I}_{8}+A{I}_{8} \\\\  & =A+A \\\\  & =2A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only statements I  and III are true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265914 :question/math-academy-id "q-130478" :question/problem "Given that $A,B,$ and $C$ are $7\\times 7$ matrices which of the following statements are always true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $(CA)B=C(BA)$\n- **II.** $(A+A)B=2AB$\n- **III.** $CB=BC$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I  is false. Matrix multiplication is associative and  $(CA)B=C(AB)$ is always true. However,  matrix multiplication is not commutative,   $AB\\ne BA$ in general. Therefore, $(CA)B\\ne C(BA)$ in general.\n\n- Statement II is true. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so $(A+A)B=AB+AB=2AB$ is always true.\n\n- Statement III is false. Matrix multiplication is not commutative in general, so $CB\\ne BC$ in general.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329640 :question/math-academy-id "q-42264" :question/problem "Given that $A,B,$ and $C$ are $2\\times 2$ matrices, which of the following statements are always true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $AB=BA$\n\n- **II.** $(AB)C=A(BC)$\n\n- **III.** $A(B-C)=(B-C)A$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. Matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $AB\\ne BA$ in general.\n\n- Statement II is true. Matrix multiplication is associative, so $(AB)C=A(BC)$ in general.\n\n- Statement III is false. Matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $A(B-C)\\ne (B-C)A$ in general.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329641 :question/math-academy-id "q-42267" :question/problem "Given that $A,B,$ and $C$ are $5\\times 5$ matrices, and  ${I}_{5}$ is the $5\\times 5$ identity matrix, which of the following statements are always true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $AB=BA$\n- **II.** $A(B+C)=AB+AC$\n- **III.** $B{I}_{5}={I}_{5}B$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. Matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $AB\\ne BA$ in general.\n\n- Statement II is true. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so $(A+B)C=AC+BC,$ and $A(B+C)=AB+AC$ in general.\n\n- Statement III is true. Matrix multiplication is not commutative in general, however, multiplication by the identity matrix is always commutative. In this case, we have  $B{I}_{5}={I}_{5}B=B.$\n\nTherefore, only statements II and III are true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345115 :question/math-academy-id "q-75662" :question/problem "Given that $A,B,$ and $C$ are $7\\times 7$ matrices, which of the following statements are always true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A(B+C)=AC+AB$\n- **II.** $A(BC)=(BA)C$\n- **III.** $AB=BA$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so \n\n$$\nA(B+C)=AB+AC=AC+AB\n$$\n\n since matrix addition is commutative.\n\n- Statement II is false. Matrix multiplication is associative, so $(AB)C=A(BC)$ in general. However, since matrix multiplication is not commutative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(BC) &=(AB)C\\ne (BA)C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $AB\\ne BA$ in general.\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345116 :question/math-academy-id "q-75664" :question/problem "Given that $A$ and $B$  are $4\\times 4$ matrices,  and ${I}_{4}$ is the $4\\times 4$ identity matrix,  which of the following statements are always true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $AB=BA$\n- **II.** $A{I}_{4}={I}_{4}A$\n- **III.** $A(B+{I}_{4})=AB+A$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. Matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $AB\\ne BA$ in general.\n\n- Statement II is true. Matrix multiplication is not commutative in general, however, multiplication by the identity matrix is always commutative. In this case, we have  $A{I}_{4}={I}_{4}A=A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so $A(B+{I}_{4})=AB+A{I}_{4}=AB+A$ in general.\n\nTherefore, only statements II  and III are true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345117 :question/math-academy-id "q-75717" :question/problem "Given that $A,B,$ and $C$ are $6\\times 6$ matrices which of the following statements are always true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $CB=BC$\n- **II.** $A(B-C)=AB-CA$\n- **III.** $A(BC)=(AB)C$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. Matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $AB\\ne BA$ in general.\n\n- Statement II is false. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so $A(B-C)=AB-AC,$ but $AC\\ne CA$ because matrix multiplication is not commutative in general.\n\n- Statement III is true. Matrix multiplication is associative and  $A(BC)=(AB)C$ is always true.\n\nTherefore, only statement  III is true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485559 :question/math-academy-id "q-75663" :question/problem "Given that $A,B,$ and $C$ are $4\\times 4$ matrices, and ${I}_{4}$ is the $4\\times 4$ identity matrix,  which of the following statements are always true?\n\nI. $A{I}_{4}={I}_{4}A$\n\nII. $(CA)B=C(AB)$\n\nIII. $BC=CB$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Matrix multiplication is not commutative in general, however, multiplication by the identity matrix is always commutative. In this case, we have  $A{I}_{4}={I}_{4}A=A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Matrix multiplication is asociative, so $(CA)B=C(AB)$ in general.\n\n- Statement III is false. Matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $BC\\ne CB$ in general.\n\nTherefore, only statements I  and II are true."}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Matrix Multiplication"}] :topic/title "Properties of Matrix Multiplication"}
